= Bayesian inference — Technical =

== Parameter posterior ==
For parameter θ and observed data y:

<pre>
p(θ|y)
= p(y|θ)p(θ) / p(y)
</pre>

with marginal likelihood:

<pre>
p(y)=∫p(y|θ)p(θ)dθ
</pre>

== Posterior predictive ==
For future observation y*:

<pre>
p(y*|y)
= ∫ p(y*|θ)p(θ|y)dθ
</pre>

which propagates parameter uncertainty into prediction.

== Model comparison ==
Bayes factors compare marginal likelihoods:

<pre>
BF₁₂ = p(y|M₁) / p(y|M₂)
</pre>

Results can be sensitive to prior mass over parameter space.

== Computation ==
Complex posteriors can require:

* Markov chain Monte Carlo;
* sequential Monte Carlo;
* variational inference;
* Laplace approximation;
* conjugate analytic methods.

Computational approximation and inferential assumptions are separate error sources.

== Model criticism ==
Posterior predictive checks, prior sensitivity analysis, calibration, and comparison to held-out data help detect mismatch.

Bayesian coherence does not guarantee model adequacy.

